import DocPage from '../../components/DocPage.astro'; import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro'; import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
---
<DocPage eyebrow="TOOL / INSPECTOR" title="Inspector" lead="Inspect a running game's semantic UI tree, node state, logs, and screenshots. Inspector is the first tool to reach for when validating an integration." description="Install, connect, and use Gua Inspector." section="inspector">
  <Callout kind="tip" title="Install Inspector"><p>Download the Windows installer <code>Gua.Inspector_*_x64-setup.exe</code> or MSI from the <a href="https://github.com/gua-project/gua/releases/tag/gua-v1.1.0">Gua 1.1.0 release assets</a>, install it, and launch Gua Inspector.</p></Callout><h2>Run and connect</h2><ol class="steps"><li><strong>Launch Inspector</strong><p>Open the installed Gua Inspector application.</p></li><li><strong>Start the in-game bridge</strong><p>Run the Gua-integrated game with its WebSocket bridge enabled.</p></li><li><strong>Enter the connection URL</strong><p>The default is <code>ws://127.0.0.1:8765</code>. Match the port configured by the game.</p></li></ol>
  <h2>Run from source (development)</h2><p>Run the following commands in the Gua repository checked out at <code>gua-v1.1.0</code>. Browser development requires Bun.</p><ol class="steps"><li><strong>Install repository dependencies</strong><CodeBlock title="PowerShell" lang="powershell" code={`bun install --frozen-lockfile
bun run --filter gua-world-tools build
bun run --filter gua-value-tools build`} /></li><li><strong>Start browser Inspector</strong><CodeBlock title="Terminal 1" lang="powershell" code={`bun run --filter @gua/inspector dev`} /></li><li><strong>Start the in-game bridge</strong><p>The Godot sample starts its bridge inside the game process. Use the sample bridge when you only need to exercise the protocol.</p><CodeBlock title="Terminal 2 (sample bridge)" lang="powershell" code={`bun run bridge:ws`} /></li><li><strong>Connect to the URL</strong><CodeBlock lang="text" code={`ws://127.0.0.1:8765`} /></li></ol>
  <h2>What to inspect</h2><div class="grid-2"><div class="mini-card"><strong>Semantic UI Tree</strong><p>Screen, role, ID, text, and parent/child structure. Confirm that the expected Controls were collected.</p></div><div class="mini-card"><strong>Node state</strong><p>Visibility, enabled state, focus, checked state, value, and bounds used by tests.</p></div><div class="mini-card"><strong>Logs</strong><p>Runtime logs published by the game, useful for state changes the connection alone cannot explain.</p></div><div class="mini-card"><strong>Screenshot</strong><p>An explicitly published PNG for comparing semantic state with the rendered frame.</p></div></div>
  <h2>Automation panel</h2><div class="grid-2"><div class="mini-card"><strong>Semantic actions</strong><p>Click, focus, set a value, check/uncheck, select, scroll, or send a key to the selected node.</p></div><div class="mini-card"><strong>Recording / Replay</strong><p>Record actions, download/import JSON, and replay. Sensitive values resolve from an in-memory secret map.</p></div><div class="mini-card"><strong>Visual compare</strong><p>Use the current screenshot or a selected PNG as a baseline and compare pixels in the browser.</p></div><div class="mini-card"><strong>Artifacts</strong><p>Explicitly download Actual, Expected, Diff images and the comparison manifest.</p></div></div>
  <Callout kind="tip" title="Browser permission model"><p>Inspector never writes arbitrary local paths implicitly. Recording and visual artifacts use download/import. Use <code>gui-mcp</code> with <code>GUA_ARTIFACT_DIR</code> when automation needs filesystem output.</p></Callout>
  <Callout kind="warn" title="Inspector does not depend on MCP"><p>Inspector connects directly to the WebSocket bridge. Starting MCP will not repair a broken Inspector connection. Check the game bridge and its port first.</p></Callout>
  <h2>Desktop development build</h2><p>Running the Tauri shell from source also requires the Rust toolchain.</p><CodeBlock title="PowerShell" lang="powershell" code={`bun run --filter @gua/inspector build\nbun run --filter @gua/inspector tauri:dev`} />
  <h2 id="codegen">Generate a C# test from a node</h2><p>Select a semantic node to generate <code>Gua.Testing</code> locators, actions, and assertions. Prefer stable IDs, check that the generated context matches your host, and review the expected state before adding it to a test. <a href="https://github.com/gua-project/gua/blob/gua-v1.1.0/packages/inspector/src/codegen.ts">Supported code-generation forms</a> · <a href="/godot/testing/#locators">Locator action waits</a>.</p>
  <h2 id="observe">Inspect additional values</h2><p>The Observe panel shows values and change notifications registered by the game when <code>observe_v1</code> is available. Enum choices come from Value catalogs. A gap or stale session requires a new subscription; Inspector does not register remote getters or promote Player to Debug. <a href="/docs/gua-runtime/#observe">Registration and lifecycle</a>.</p>
  <h2 id="trace">Open Gua Trace offline</h2><p>Use <strong>Open Gua Trace (offline)</strong> to read a saved Trace without connecting to a game. Inspect operations, host completion, observations, the runner's outcome, and explicit attachments. Trace is not Recording and does not execute a replay. <code>Gua.Testing.GuaTraceReport.WriteHtml</code> creates a self-contained HTML report without Node or Bun. <a href="/godot/testing/#trace">Create a Trace and report</a>.</p>
</DocPage>
